//! # Actors
//!
//! Actors own mutable state and process one mailbox message at a time.
//! Declare handlers with `receive fn`; a handler with no return type is a
//! fire-and-forget send, while a handler returning `R` is an ask whose call
//! returns `Result<R, AskError>` after `await`.
//!
//! ## Declaration, spawn, local dispatch, and ask
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Counter {
//!     var count: i64 = 0;
//!     receive fn increment(n: i64) { count = count + n; }
//!     receive fn total() -> i64 { count }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let c = spawn Counter(count: 0);
//!     c.increment(10);
//!     c.increment(20);
//!     let result = await c.total();
//!     match result {
//!         Ok(value) => println(f"total={value}"),
//!         Err(_) => println("ask failed"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `spawn Counter(...)` returns a typed `LocalPid<Counter>`. Calling a
//! no-result receive function dispatches locally and has type `()`. Calling a
//! result-bearing receive function creates a request; `await` yields the
//! `Result`, never the bare reply.
//!
//! ## Actor-to-actor dispatch
//!
//! Store the dependency as `LocalPid<Worker>`, spawn it before its consumer,
//! and await its handler from the consumer:
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Worker {
//!     let factor: i64;
//!     receive fn compute(n: i64) -> i64 { n * factor }
//! }
//! actor Coordinator {
//!     let worker: LocalPid<Worker>;
//!     receive fn run(n: i64) -> i64 {
//!         let reply = await worker.compute(n);
//!         match reply {
//!             Ok(value) => value,
//!             Err(_) => -1,
//!         }
//!     }
//! }
//! fn main() {
//!     let worker = spawn Worker(factor: 6);
//!     let coordinator = spawn Coordinator(worker: worker);
//!     match await coordinator.run(7) {
//!         Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         Err(_) => println("failed"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! An actor handle is an opaque capability, not a mailbox receiver. Opaque
//! handles cannot be used as `receive fn` parameters; use a typed actor PID
//! such as `LocalPid<Worker>` instead. Lambda actors accept one message type;
//! use a declared actor when several handlers or supervision are required.
